WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Speaker 1: Well, what a packed program I have&nbsp;
for you today, starting with economic news.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:11.040
As predicted, it completely cluelessly by me&nbsp;
feeling around the dark and knowing, feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:17.040
around in the dark and knowing absolutely nothing&nbsp;
about anything. Inflation has declined. We now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.920
have seen a lower than expected inflation number&nbsp;
this morning. This is what I've been saying for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:29.820
months and since we got the June numbers in July.&nbsp;
Inflation year over year. After the June numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:40.500
we received in July were at 9.1%. Now we are down&nbsp;
to 8.5% coming in below the expected 8.7%. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:47.100
the way that corporate media reports these&nbsp;
numbers continues to be so confusing. Look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:58.440
this headline from CNBC. Consumer prices rose 8.5%&nbsp;
in July, less than expected as inflation pressures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:05.280
ease a bit. This is good news, but the reporting&nbsp;
makes it sound like in July of this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:15.720
prices went up 8.5%. That is not the case. What&nbsp;
this number means is that for the 12 month period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:23.940
ending in July, prices are up 8.8. 5%. So another&nbsp;
way to think about it would be in July, we got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:32.700
the inflation numbers from June 2021 through&nbsp;
June 2022, and that was 9.1%. Now we are adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:40.080
the numbers from July 22 and removing the numbers&nbsp;
from June 22. So this new number is for July 2021&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:46.620
through July 2022. There must be some simpler&nbsp;
way to report this so that people don't get as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:52.860
confused as they get. But the important thing is&nbsp;
this is good news for an economy that otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.860 --> 00:02:00.360
looks quite good, essentially full employment, gas&nbsp;
prices, declining stock market, at least over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:06.060
last six weeks, looking significantly better than&nbsp;
it had looked in the first months of 2022. A lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:11.220
of these things look good again, considering that&nbsp;
these are the traditional economic indicators. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:16.440
if we continue to see an inflation decline,&nbsp;
which I expect that we will as as gas prices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:22.080
in the first ten days of August has continued&nbsp;
down, that is going to be a very good thing. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:31.200
one other important bit of news here. Real average&nbsp;
hourly earnings for all employees went up 0.5% in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.760
July. So this is another element that makes the&nbsp;
economy look relatively solid, which is not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:42.660
did we see inflation decline, we also saw wages&nbsp;
go up, which this concern about buying power for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:49.440
the average American is mitigated to some degree&nbsp;
by prices down and wages up. Now, is there still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.520
poverty? Of course. Is there still hunger? Of&nbsp;
course. Inequality, of course. This doesn't mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.900
we've solved the economic problems that we have&nbsp;
and that are inherent to the economic system we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:03.000
have. But by the indicators that we pay attention&nbsp;
to, this is a significant improvement. Now, what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.280
the right wing story going to be? I don't know. I&nbsp;
mean, listen, inflation's down. Stock markets are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:14.280
up. Gas prices are down. Hourly earnings are up.&nbsp;
Somehow it must be very bad for the country and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:19.080
for Joe Biden. I don't know what they're going to&nbsp;
say probably. Well, inflation is still too high.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.220
Yeah, okay. But it's lower than expected and it's&nbsp;
declining. So we'll see what they come up with

